# reintroduce CVE-2025-66416 (see phase-800 note in requirements.txt). protobuf
# there is only exercised by semgrep/OTEL tooling on trusted repo content.
# Re-checked 2026-07-30 at semgrep 1.172.0 (latest on PyPI, well past the
# OTEL>=1.37 condition): opentelemetry-api/-sdk are now ~=1.37.0 as hoped, but
# semgrep still hard-pins mcp==1.23.3 — the actual blocker is unchanged.
# Re-review at next expiry: check again for a semgrep release with mcp>=1.28.
CVE-2026-0994 exp:2026-08-13

# GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964: msgpack for Python out-of-bounds read/crash on malformed
# input (fixed in 1.2.1). Present in every first-party Python image.
# Why no fix (re-investigated 2026-08-06, Phase 818): this is NOT a dependency
# we install — `pip show msgpack` finds nothing. It is pip's OWN vendored copy:
# /usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/msgpack (and a second
# copy inside the ensurepip bundled wheel). Even the latest pip — 26.2.1, which
# the images install via `pip install --upgrade pip` — still vendors msgpack
# 1.1.2. The requirements-level `msgpack>=1.2.1` pins are real and effective for
# any genuine top-level use, but they cannot touch pip's internal _vendor tree.
# pip (26.2.1, the current release, installed via `pip install --upgrade pip`)
# still vendors the old msgpack, so it is present in every image regardless of
# the base Python patch level. Only a future pip that re-vendors msgpack >= 1.2.1
# clears this. The sibling CVE-2025-47273/setuptools is the same pip-vendored
# situation; both are exceptioned below.
# Why not exploitable: pip's vendored msgpack is used only by pip's own
# resolver/cache machinery at install time on trusted inputs; no application or
# runtime code path in any image imports it, and pip is not run at runtime.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 exp:2026-08-13

# CVE-2025-47273: setuptools path traversal in PackageIndex (fixed in 78.1.1).
# Same root cause as GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 above: NOT a dependency we install
# (`pip show setuptools` finds the live one is current) — this is pip's own
# vendored copy, pip/_vendor/pkg_resources, which Trivy reports as an old
# setuptools (~70.3.0). Present in pip 26.2.1's _vendor tree and the ensurepip
# bundled wheel. Re-investigated 2026-08-06 (Phase 818): the build is
# non-deterministic about whether Trivy surfaces this or the sibling msgpack
# CVE first, so both are exceptioned. Neither is fixable by us — only a future
# pip release that re-vendors these clears them.
# Why not exploitable: pip's vendored pkg_resources is used only by pip's own
# install-time machinery on trusted inputs; no application or runtime code path
# imports it, and pip is not run at runtime.
CVE-2025-47273 exp:2026-08-13



# GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf: gRPC-Go xDS RBAC and HTTP/2 Vulnerabilities (fixed in
# grpc-go v1.82.1). Re-verified 2026-08-03: prometheus was bumped to v3.13.2
# in this same review, which picks up the fix (scanned clean, 0 findings) —
# removed from the affected list below. Still no upstream release of
# alertmanager (v0.33.1, latest), grafana (13.0.4-ubuntu, latest — checked
# 13.1.1-ubuntu too, still vulnerable plus a larger CVE set, not worth
# switching), loki (3.7.4, latest), promtail (v3.6.11, latest — EOL project),
# or gcr.io/cadvisor/cadvisor (v0.52.1 — checked v0.55.1, carries far more
# HIGH/CRITICAL CVEs, not an improvement) has picked up the fix.
# Why not exploitable here: the vulnerable code paths are gRPC's xDS RBAC
# filter and an HTTP/2 handling flaw used by Envoy-style xDS control planes.
# None of these five images run as xDS servers or accept untrusted gRPC/xDS
# input in our deployment — they're monitoring/logging tools sitting behind
# the proxy, reachable only from trusted internal callers.
GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf exp:2026-08-10

# ── gcr.io/cadvisor/cadvisor + prom/haproxy-exporter — Phase 810 ──
# Phase 810 wired these two images (plus tecnativa/docker-socket-proxy) into
# the scan gate for the first time: TRIVY_IMAGES was a hand-maintained list
# that had silently drifted from docker-compose.monitoring.yml and never
# included them, so they went unscanned indefinitely. tecnativa's bump
# (0.3.0 -> v0.5.0) scans completely clean, no waiver needed. cadvisor was
# bumped v0.47.2 -> v0.52.1 (57 findings -> 41) and haproxy-exporter has no
# newer tag to bump to — prom/haproxy-exporter:v0.15.0 (2023-02-15) is the
# last version the upstream project ever published; it appears abandoned.
# A proper fix for haproxy-exporter is replacing it with HAProxy 2.8's
# built-in Prometheus exporter (removes the abandoned sidecar entirely) —
# tracked as a follow-up phase, not done here. Neither image is exposed on
# any host port (no `ports:` mapping in docker-compose.monitoring.yml) — both
# are reachable only from Prometheus/other containers on the internal
# `ja4proxy-monitoring` Docker network, never externally or from the host.
#
# The block below groups the Go stdlib / x/crypto / x/net / x/oauth2 DoS and
# parsing-bug backlog shared by both images (cadvisor's Go 1.24.1/x-pkg
# vintage and haproxy-exporter's much older abandoned Go 1.19.5/x-pkg
# vintage) — all denial-of-service or memory-safety bugs in HTTP/TLS/HTML/URL
# parsing paths, none of them RCE. Why not exploitable: cadvisor serves only
# a Prometheus metrics scrape endpoint and reads /proc, /sys, and the
# read-only Docker socket — it never parses untrusted HTML, arbitrary URLs,
# or terminates TLS from an untrusted peer. haproxy-exporter only scrapes
# HAProxy's own local stats page over plain HTTP inside the internal
# network — no untrusted input reaches these code paths in either.
# Re-verified 2026-08-03 (only the entries actually re-triggered by this
# scan run got their exp: date bumped; the rest of the batch below is inert
# until it recurs): cadvisor v0.55.1 and haproxy-exporter's abandoned
# v0.15.0 (still the newest tag published, 2023-02-15) offer no improvement.
